WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.340
Hey Crazies.

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:05.040
Cosmic time is measured in billions of years.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.900
That’s thousands of millions.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.100
What? Huh? What?!

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:15.860
When we hear words like million or billion, all we really hear is big.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.920
So let’s get a better perspective with a different unit of time:

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.680
the galactic year.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.040
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.440
What’s a galactic year?

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.520
The time it takes the Sun to orbit the galaxy.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.340
It’s about 230 million years.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.380
Can we just make up a unit like that?

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.860
Well yeah. All units are made up.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.440
Alright, let’s start with some basics.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.480
Any measurement has two required parts:

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.700
A number and a unit.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.360
You need both or it’s not a measurement.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.940
And don’t give me any of that “the unit is implied” non-sense.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.900
Anyway, a measurement is whatever a measurement is.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:04.380
The distance between the Sun and Earth is an average of about 93 million miles.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:09.100
Miles is the unit and 93 million is the number,

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.040
but it doesn’t matter what number and unit we assign to this distance.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.860
I could just as easily say this is 150 million kilometers

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.100
or 150 gigameters.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.220
None of that actually changes the distance between the Earth and the Sun.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.840
The point is the unit choice is up to us, so we might as well chose one that’s convenient.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:35.080
Since the measurement is whatever it is, using a larger unit means we get a smaller number.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.820
So let’s make the unit here really big.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:42.799
We can call this distance 1 astronomical unit or A.U.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.080
We can just do that?

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.620
Oh yeah, for sure.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.700
All units are made up.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:53.400
Calling the Earth-Sun distance 1 A.U. is convenient for distances within our solar system.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.680
Mars is 1.5 A.U. from the Sun.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.040
Jupiter? 5.2 A.U.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.400
Neptune? 30 A.U.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.480
Check out this video, if you want to learn more about the scale of the solar system.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.000
Ok, we just chose a convenient unit for distance.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.040
We can do the same with time.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.420
There are a lot of different units for time.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:19.280
The standard base unit is seconds, but that’s way too small for measuring cosmic time.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.380
We’ve also got minutes, hours, days, months, and even years,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.540
but they’re still not big enough.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.500
Remember, cosmic time is measured in thousands of millions of years.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.420
A million years, or what we’d call a mega-year, is still a little too small.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.680
Some of our numbers will be uncomfortably large.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.280
A billion years, or what we’d call a giga-year, has the opposite problem.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.120
Some of our numbers will end up a little too small.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.200
We need a unit that’s in-between the two and that's where the galactic year come in.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.600
The time it takes for the Earth to travel around the Sun once is defined as 1 year.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.140
For the rest of the video, we’ll call that one Earth-year.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:07.700
The time it takes for the Sun to travel around the galaxy once is about 230 million Earth-years.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.520
We call that one galactic year.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.720
It’s like a sweet spot between a million and a billion.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.700
It’s exactly what we need.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:20.980
The beginning of the known universe was 13.8 billion Earth-years ago,

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.360
which is equivalent to 60 galactic years ago.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:30.560
That’s it. That’s the number. Not 60 million. Not 60 thousand. Just 60.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.320
Is that cool or what?!

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:38.600
We’ve made our unit just the right size so that our numbers are ordinary everyday numbers.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:42.200
So let’s see what happens with the rest of cosmic time.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:46.940
Galaxies begin to form about 2 galactic years after the big bang.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.900
That’s about 58 galactic years ago.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.960
Our own Sun is a third generation star,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.340
so it didn’t form for another 36.3 galactic years,

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.780
about 21.7 galactic years ago.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.400
The Earth formed shortly after, about 20 galactic years ago.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.220
Some things still happen quickly though. I don’t think you can keep using years.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.680
Ok, then we’ll subdivide it.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:13.560
Fine!

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.040
If we’re calling this a year,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.300
then we can easily divide it the same way we divide a normal Earth-year.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:24.200
Months vary a bit too much, so we’ll skip those and jump right to days.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.400
Let’s say there are 365 galactic days in a galactic year,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.060
and 24 galactic hours in a galactic day,

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.640
and 60 galactic minutes in a galactic hour, and

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.760
and so on.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.120
That way we can maintain the same perspective we had with galactic years.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.840
It might have only taken 2 galactic years for galaxies to form,

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.980
but it only took about 14 galactic hours for the universe to go transparent.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:51.940
That’s how early the cosmic microwave background was released.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.900
Way before galaxies!

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.860
The universe was dark for a long time.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:58.960
Can we do geologic time too?

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.760
Sure. Why not?

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.880
The Earth formed 20 galactic years ago.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:10.220
The Moon formed about 160 galactic days later, so not long after.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:15.800
The Earth spent a few galactic years being continuously bombarded with space rocks.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:22.480
Around 15 galactic years ago, that bombardment slowed just enough for life to form.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:26.320
Seriously. Life formed the moment it was possible.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.500
By 14 galactic years ago, photosynthesis had evolved in cyanobacteria.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:36.460
By 11 galactic years ago, some living cells had formed a cell nucleus.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.180
At this point, Earth still only has single-celled life.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:44.760
Multicellular organisms don’t appear until three galactic years ago.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.240
Apparently, that was a rough transition.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:51.020
After that though, life got pretty good at this whole evolution thing.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.800
By two galactic years ago, there were plants on land.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.920
By one and half galactic years ago, there were animals on land.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:05.600
The dinosaurs went extinct less than a galactic year ago, about 103 galactic days ago.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.620
Modern humans evolved about 7.5 galactic hours ago,

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:15.360
human civilization began just 27 galactic minutes ago,

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.720
and a single human life is only about 12 galactic seconds long.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.280
Whoa.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.800
We are such a tiny fraction of cosmic time

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.840
and that’s only the time that’s happened so far.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:35.300
Four galactic years from now, the Sun will be so hot that the Earth’s oceans will boil away.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:41.340
In about 22 galactic years, the Sun will expand into a red giant engulfing the Earth.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:45.740
Any record we ever existed here will be erased from the universe.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.580
That got depressing real fast.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:53.640
As you can see though, galactic years is an extremely convenient unit for cosmic time.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:58.900
For quicker jumps, we can easily divide it up into smaller units to get bigger numbers.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.920
But, galactic years works quite well most of the time

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:06.640
and gives us a really good perspective on cosmic and geologic time scales.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:11.600
It keeps the numbers for all these events as ordinary everyday numbers.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:14.340
They’re numbers we have a good handle on.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.180
So, hopefully that helped with your perspective a little bit.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:19.860
If you have any questions, please ask in the comments.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.820
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:24.440
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:28.360
And until next time, remember, it’s ok to be a little crazy.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:38.020
Why did you only use straight paths for the photon if all paths are possible?

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:39.419
What about the curved ones?

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:42.420
Ok, yes, curved paths are possible.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.580
It’s just that they’re difficult to animate,

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:46.740
especially with the phasor arrows.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.680
I only needed a few paths to make my point, so I chose the easy ones.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:52.380
Anyway, thanks for watching.

